          "ruleId": "owasp-audit/CVE-2020-28168/package-lock.json/axios",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Needs validation: axios 0.21.0 (lockfile marks it \"Critical security vulnerability fixed in v0.21.1\") is used on a user-controlled URL; a redirect may bypass an egress proxy. Blockers: Whether the production runtime routes outbound HTTP through an egress proxy (HTTP_PROXY/HTTPS_PROXY, which axios 0.21.0 honours by default) that is relied on to block internal or metadata destinations; Whether the installed axios 0.21.0 sends a cross-host redirect hop directly instead of through the configured proxy (the CVE-2020-28168 behaviour). Validation plan: 1) In the production container: `printenv | grep -iE '^(https?|no)_proxy='`. If nothing prints, there is no proxy control. Close this as rejected, ruled out by the absence of a proxy (the direct-SSRF finding still applies). 2) If a proxy is set: run `npm ls axios` (expect axios@0.21.0) and `npm audit --omit=dev`. Then, locally, point HTTP_PROXY at a stub proxy that answers every request with a 302 to http://127.0.0.1:9999/internal, run a stub server on 9999, and call require('axios').get('http://preview.test/'). Confirmed: the stub on 9999 receives the request directly, so the redirect hop skipped the proxy. Rejected: the proxy receives the redirected request for 127.0.0.1:9999/internal. Upgrading axios (>= 0.21.1, ideally current 1.x) closes this either way."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "package-lock.json",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
